1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,599
This lake in Alaska is leaking
a gas you can set on fire.

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:05,132
Bubbles rise from the bottom.

3
00:00:05,201 --> 00:00:08,081
A scientist catches the gas
and holds a flame to it.

4
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:09,903
It burns. It is methane.

5
00:00:10,019 --> 00:00:13,189
And it is coming from ground
that is supposed to stay frozen.

6
00:00:13,247 --> 00:00:16,660
This is Big Trail Lake,
outside Fairbanks, Alaska.

7
00:00:16,904 --> 00:00:21,792
Under most of Alaska and northern
Canada lies permafrost: soil,

8
00:00:21,862 --> 00:00:26,691
ice or plant material that has stayed
frozen for at least two years in a row.

9
00:00:26,807 --> 00:00:30,244
On top sits a thin active layer
that thaws every summer.

10
00:00:30,418 --> 00:00:32,937
To see what is down there,
you can walk into it.

11
00:00:33,181 --> 00:00:37,001
The U.S. Army Corps of Engineers
runs a research tunnel dug straight

12
00:00:37,047 --> 00:00:39,125
into the frozen ground near Fairbanks.

13
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:42,202
The walls are frozen mud, roots and ice.

14
00:00:42,318 --> 00:00:45,476
In interior Alaska,
that ice comes in huge wedges.

15
00:00:45,557 --> 00:00:47,276
Some are as big as garages.

16
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:48,924
And the Arctic is warming fast.

17
00:00:48,947 --> 00:00:52,663
Temperatures in the Arctic and boreal regions
are rising nearly four times as fast

18
00:00:52,697 --> 00:00:53,800
as in the mid-latitudes.

19
00:00:53,835 --> 00:00:56,564
When an ice wedge melts,
the ground above it collapses.

20
00:00:56,598 --> 00:00:59,187
The hole fills with water,
and a new pond is born.

21
00:00:59,257 --> 00:01:01,266
Scientists call it a thermokarst pond.

22
00:01:01,312 --> 00:01:02,589
Then microbes go to work.

23
00:01:02,612 --> 00:01:05,352
They feast on organic material
that was frozen in the ground,

24
00:01:05,375 --> 00:01:09,079
and they release methane, a potent
greenhouse gas that also burns.

25
00:01:09,172 --> 00:01:12,968
At this lake, the emissions are about ten
times higher than the rest of the lakes

26
00:01:13,026 --> 00:01:14,350
and wetlands in the Arctic.

27
00:01:14,373 --> 00:01:16,138
The fuel is thawing permafrost.

28
00:01:16,161 --> 00:01:18,843
So how do you track lakes
like this across the Arctic?

29
00:01:19,017 --> 00:01:23,661
From above. NASA's Arctic Boreal
Vulnerability Experiment, called ABoVE,

30
00:01:23,696 --> 00:01:28,711
has been studying Alaska and northwestern
Canada since 2015 with satellites,

31
00:01:28,758 --> 00:01:30,522
aircraft and teams on the ground.

32
00:01:30,801 --> 00:01:32,415
One of its tools is UAVSAR,

33
00:01:32,462 --> 00:01:36,258
a radar that weighs nearly one thousand
pounds and hangs under a NASA jet.

34
00:01:36,409 --> 00:01:37,965
Radar can see through clouds.

35
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,970
It measures soil moisture, vegetation
and permafrost on the ground below.

36
00:01:41,993 --> 00:01:46,428
It is so precise that pilots use a special
system to fly the exact same path year

37
00:01:46,487 --> 00:01:49,215
after year, so the team
can see how the land is changing.

38
00:01:49,366 --> 00:01:50,805
Fire makes all of this worse.

39
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:54,242
These northern forests are adapted
to burn about once a century.

40
00:01:54,288 --> 00:01:56,053
But on the forest floor sits duff,

41
00:01:56,088 --> 00:02:00,070
a layer of debris that builds up
about one inch every seventeen years.

42
00:02:00,314 --> 00:02:03,367
In warm, dry summers it dries
out fast and becomes fuel.

43
00:02:03,413 --> 00:02:06,815
When a fire burns that layer, it
is like taking the lid off a cooler.

44
00:02:06,908 --> 00:02:08,963
The frozen ground below starts to thaw.

45
00:02:09,033 --> 00:02:13,317
The fire releases carbon, the thawing
ground releases more, the air gets warmer,

46
00:02:13,352 --> 00:02:14,861
and the cycle feeds itself.

47
00:02:14,861 --> 00:02:16,997
That is why NASA keeps flying over Alaska.

48
00:02:17,032 --> 00:02:19,528
What happens in the Arctic
does not stay in the Arctic.

49
00:02:19,575 --> 00:02:23,777
For another place that changes when you
see it from above, watch this one next.
